# 3.1 System Requirements Specification (SRS)
## 3.1.1 System Actors
### a. Guest User (Unauthenticated User)
A Guest User is a visitor who accesses the system without a valid authentication session. This actor can only access public pages and authentication-related functions. Guest Users are not allowed to access personal nutrition data or protected system features.
Main responsibilities of a Guest User include:
* Viewing the landing page and public information.
* Creating a new account through the registration process.
* Logging into the system using a valid email and password.
* Being redirected to the appropriate page after successful authentication.
Restricted features:
* Dashboard
* Food Search
* Meal Logging
* Profile Management
* Reports and Analytics
* Weight Tracking

### b. Authenticated User
An Authenticated User is the primary actor of the HealthyBite system. After successful authentication, users can access personalized nutrition management features.
If a user has not completed the onboarding process, the system redirects them to the health profile setup workflow before granting access to the Dashboard.
Main responsibilities of an Authenticated User include:
* Managing authentication sessions.
* Completing the onboarding process.
* Managing personal health profiles.
* Managing nutrition and health goals.
* Searching for food and nutrition information.
* Recording daily meal logs.
* Monitoring daily nutrition intake through the Dashboard.
* Viewing reports and body progress information.
* Recording and managing weight logs.
* Logging out of the system.

---

## 3.1.2 Functional Requirements
### FR-01 User Registration and Authentication
The system shall allow users to create a new account using basic registration information.
The system shall allow registered users to authenticate using their email and password.
Upon successful authentication:
* The backend shall generate an access token.
* The system shall support refresh token mechanisms for session maintenance.
* Protected resources shall only be accessible to authenticated users.

### FR-02 Health Profile Onboarding
The system shall provide an onboarding workflow to collect initial health information from users.
Collected information may include:
* Gender
* Age
* Height
* Weight
* Activity Level
* Nutrition Goal
The collected information shall be used to support nutrition calculations and personalized recommendations.

### FR-03 Profile Management
The system shall allow authenticated users to:
* View personal profile information.
* Update health-related information.
* Maintain nutrition-related settings.
The profile data shall be stored and synchronized with the backend database.

### FR-04 Goal Management
The system shall allow users to define and update nutrition goals.
Supported goals may include:
* Weight Maintenance
* Weight Loss
* Weight Gain
The goal configuration shall be used in nutrition target calculations.

### FR-05 Food Search
The system shall allow users to search for food items and nutrition information.
The system may retrieve nutrition data from:
* Local nutrition datasets.
* External food data providers.
* Internal food databases.
Food search results shall include calories and macronutrient information when available.

### FR-06 Meal Loggin
The system shall allow users to record foods consumed during daily meals.
Supported meal categories include:
* Breakfast
* Lunch
* Dinner
* Snacks
The system shall calculate and store:
* Total Calories
* Protein
* Carbohydrates
* Fat
for each meal record.

### FR-07 Nutrition Dashboard
The system shall provide a dashboard that summarizes daily nutrition information.
The dashboard shall display:
* Daily calorie intake
* Protein consumption
* Carbohydrate consumption
* Fat consumption
* Progress toward nutrition goals
Dashboard data shall be generated from profile and meal log information.

### FR-08 Reports and Body Progress Tracking
The system shall provide reporting features to help users monitor nutrition and health progress.
The system may provide:
* Nutrition summaries
* Historical meal information
* Weight tracking history
* Health progress indicators

### FR-09 Weight Log Management
The system shall allow users to:
* Record body weight measurements.
* View historical weight records.
* Delete incorrect weight entries.
Weight data shall be used to support body progress monitoring.
---

## 3.1.3 Non-Functional Requirements
### NFR-01 Security
The system shall ensure the confidentiality of user information and health-related data.
Security requirements include:
* Protected APIs must require authentication.
* Access tokens shall be used for authorization.
* Refresh tokens shall be managed securely.
* Sensitive credentials shall not be exposed to clients.
* User passwords shall be stored using secure hashing mechanisms.

### NFR-02 Data Integrity
The system shall maintain data consistency and accuracy across all modules.
Requirements include:
* Health profile information must be stored accurately.
* Meal logs and meal details must remain synchronized.
* Nutrition calculations must use valid and complete data.
* Database transactions should prevent partial or inconsistent updates.

### NFR-03 Performance
The system should provide acceptable response times under normal operating conditions.
Expected targets:
* Authentication requests should complete within a few seconds.
* Food search requests should return results with minimal delay.
* Dashboard data should be generated efficiently.

### NFR-04 Scalability
The system shall follow a modular architecture to support future expansion.
Potential future enhancements include:
* Additional food data providers.
* AI-based nutrition recommendations.
* Barcode scanning integration.
* Mobile application development.
* Advanced nutrition analytics.
* AI nutrition assistant features.

### NFR-05 Availability
The system should remain operational and accessible whenever required by users.
Deployment infrastructure should support:
* Health monitoring.
* Service recovery mechanisms.
* Containerized deployment using Docker.

### NFR-06 Maintainability
The system shall be designed using a layered architecture to improve maintainability.
The source code should follow:
* Separation of concerns.
* Reusable service components.
* Consistent coding conventions.
* Clear documentation and project structure.
